1
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:07,778
Pulse groupers. How records become the rows of a result.

2
00:00:08,578 --> 00:00:17,439
By default, groupers run fourth, after the attributes, so a derived column can be a key. The aggregations then fold inside each bucket.

3
00:00:18,239 --> 00:00:24,796
An entry names a type and a field, plus an interval, parameters or an include list where the grouper takes them.

4
00:00:25,596 --> 00:00:34,735
Each output row carries its key under the field's name. Leave groups empty and the whole cohort is one bucket: three hundred orders in a single row.

5
00:00:35,535 --> 00:00:39,127
Group by region instead, and you get one row per region.

6
00:00:39,927 --> 00:00:44,628
Name a field and leave out the type, and Pulse picks the grouper from the field's type.

7
00:00:45,428 --> 00:00:55,527
A number gets fixed-width bands of ten. A category gets one bucket per value. A date or datetime is bucketed by day, and a multi-select by option.

8
00:00:56,327 --> 00:01:04,783
An explicit type always wins. Predict lists every slot it filled under defaults applied, with the rule it used, before anything runs.

9
00:01:05,583 --> 00:01:16,812
Numbers can be banded several ways today. The range grouper cuts fixed-width bands, here forty wide, and keys each band low to high. The upper edge belongs to the next band.

10
00:01:17,612 --> 00:01:21,929
The rounded grouper makes the same cut, but keys each band by its floor.

11
00:01:22,729 --> 00:01:31,697
The numeric ranges grouper takes uneven named bands, in the order you list them. A value outside every range lands in unmatched, last.

12
00:01:32,497 --> 00:01:42,020
The quantile grouper cuts equal-count bands: four quartiles of seventy-two orders. It buffers the whole input, and its rows come out in bucket order.

13
00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:46,967
The date grouper buckets by a calendar component, from the hour to the year.

14
00:01:47,767 --> 00:01:53,876
Day of week runs Monday first, in every output. In this cohort, Saturday is the busiest day.

15
00:01:54,676 --> 00:02:02,599
The date ranges grouper takes named periods. Rows keep the order you supplied, and every other day lands in unmatched, last.

16
00:02:03,399 --> 00:02:17,380
A multi-select can be read per option or per combination. The per-element grouper gives one bucket per option. An order that ticked web and app counts in both, so the buckets add up to more than the orders. That is correct.

17
00:02:18,180 --> 00:02:24,439
The set value grouper gives one bucket per exact combination instead, keyed by the sorted labels.

18
00:02:25,239 --> 00:02:34,314
Ticking nothing is a real answer. The combination grouper keeps it as an empty key, and the per-option grouper skips it. Neither counts it as null.

19
00:02:35,114 --> 00:02:39,837
A blank is different: no answer at all. It gets no bucket and counts as null.

20
00:02:40,637 --> 00:02:45,722
List two groupers, and each row is keyed by both: a tuple of region and plan.

21
00:02:46,522 --> 00:02:56,109
You get one row per combination that was actually observed. No order in the West bought Pro, so there is no West Pro row: eleven rows, not twelve.

22
00:02:56,909 --> 00:03:03,253
Rows come out in each grouper's own key order, outer to inner, and every row carries both key columns.

23
00:03:04,053 --> 00:03:07,304
A null at any key drops the record from every row.

24
00:03:08,104 --> 00:03:15,216
Ten orders have no region and five have no plan, so the rows hold two hundred and eighty-five of the three hundred.

25
00:03:16,016 --> 00:03:21,997
Each grouper still counts its own nulls once, as it would alone, and each group key is the full tuple.

26
00:03:22,797 --> 00:03:30,528
Fan-out multiplies. Group by plan, then by channel, and each order lands once per option it ticked, inside its plan.

27
00:03:31,328 --> 00:03:35,901
Two entries on the same key column are refused, with a validation error.

28
00:03:36,701 --> 00:03:45,477
Before a run, predict estimates the groups as the product of each grouper's estimate: four regions times three plans is twelve, an upper bound.

29
00:03:46,277 --> 00:03:53,624
The run enforces the limit on the groups it actually meets, and refuses with a limit exceeded error once they pass it.

30
00:03:54,424 --> 00:04:04,224
Quantiles and k-means fit once over every filtered record by default. Set fit to within, and they refit inside each bucket of the groupers before them.

31
00:04:05,024 --> 00:04:09,213
An include list allow-lists keys, and it also fixes their order.

32
00:04:10,013 --> 00:04:14,629
Ask for West, then North, and that is exactly what comes back, in that order.

33
00:04:15,429 --> 00:04:24,632
Every other record is skipped on the same path as a null, so the null count reads one hundred and fifty-one. A listed key with no records still drops.

34
00:04:25,432 --> 00:04:32,949
Today include works on the category, date, date range and multi-select groupers. For number bands, filter the values.

35
00:04:33,749 --> 00:04:42,248
Every grouper reports the same floor in its components: total n, the records it placed in a bucket, and n null, the ones it couldn't place.

36
00:04:43,048 --> 00:04:52,059
An aggregation counts differently. East has sixty-one orders, but only fifty-seven have a spend, so its average reads fifty-seven values.

37
00:04:52,859 --> 00:04:59,075
A grouper counts every record after the filters. An aggregation counts only the values it used.

38
00:04:59,875 --> 00:05:08,139
The composite rows carry through the rest of the request. Sort, return, windows, post-tests and grouped matrices all follow them.

39
00:05:08,939 --> 00:05:13,363
Tier-one tests run once per group, and each result names its group key.

40
00:05:14,163 --> 00:05:23,195
Series overlays run once per partition, over the leading keys, so put the date grouper last. Year over year needs exactly one date grouper.

41
00:05:23,995 --> 00:05:32,195
A grouper can key on a window's label too. Here a row number, counted per customer in time order, numbers each customer's visits.

42
00:05:32,995 --> 00:05:37,163
Named ranges on that number split each first visit from the repeats.

43
00:05:37,963 --> 00:05:42,238
That gives eighty-seven first orders and two hundred and thirteen repeats.

44
00:05:43,038 --> 00:05:50,939
Because the grouper reads a window label, the windows run first, over the filtered records. Predict reports the plan before the run.

45
00:05:51,739 --> 00:05:56,547
Datetimes are stored as UTC instants, but their buckets read a calendar.

46
00:05:57,347 --> 00:06:06,528
Read in UTC, Monday has thirty-eight orders. Read each order on its own store's calendar, through a zone field, and Monday has forty-seven.

47
00:06:07,328 --> 00:06:14,248
A slot or a whole request can set the zone. A row whose zone is null gets no bucket, and counts as null.

48
00:06:15,048 --> 00:06:26,299
Those are the groupers today: categories, number bands, quantiles, calendar parts and named periods, k-means clusters, and a multi-select read per option or per combination.

49
00:06:27,099 --> 00:06:36,600
More groupers are added over time, and embedders can register their own through the extensions option. The manifest always shows what your instance offers.

50
00:06:37,400 --> 00:06:42,571
Remember: check what each key dropped before you read the rows. Next up, aggregators.

51
00:06:43,371 --> 00:06:44,616
Pulse groupers.
